\title{Crossing count and ordinary crossing number}
\taxon{Definition}
\tag{definition}
\meta{defines}{crossing count; ordinary crossing number}
\meta{dependencies}{[Admissible drawings](cn-0004)}
\meta{provenance}{[OpenAI, 23 September 2026](cn-0042), Section 1; reconstructed as a reading account on 9 October 2026. [Lean source: DrawingDefinition](/source-material/openai-math/lean/OAI/Combinatorics/Crossing/DrawingDefinition.lean).}

\p{For an [admissible drawing](cn-0004) #{D}, its \em{crossing count} #{c(D)} is the number of points belonging to two distinct edge interiors. Every crossing point is counted: a pair that crosses three times contributes three. This is neither a count of crossing edge pairs nor a parity count. See [Pach–Tóth](cn-0053) for the distinctions between these invariants.}

\p{The \em{ordinary crossing number} is}

##{\operatorname{cr}(G)=\min\{c(D):D\text{ is an admissible drawing of }G\}.}

\p{For the complete bipartite graphs studied here, [the axis construction](cn-0036) supplies an admissible drawing. The attainable counts form a nonempty subset of the nonnegative integers, so their least element is attained. The Lean definition uses the infimum of this set; existence and attainment are proved separately.}

\p{Counting only crossings between edges with no common endpoint gives the separately defined [independent crossing number](cn-0066).}
